//? Title: Arnoldi Iteration: Eigenvalues From Matrix-Vector Products
//? Subtitle: Krylov subspaces, the Hessenberg projection and Ritz pairs, used to find the slow modes of a random walk on the handwritten digits with datamunge's algorithms::arnoldi_iteration
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Arnoldi iteration finds eigenvalues of a large matrix \(A\) using nothing but products
\(x\mapsto Ax\). After \(m\) products it holds an orthonormal basis of the Krylov subspace
\(\mathrm{span}\{b,Ab,\dots,A^{m-1}b\}\) and a small upper-Hessenberg matrix \(H_m\), the
projection of \(A\) onto that subspace. The eigenvalues of \(H_m\), the *Ritz values*,
approximate the extreme eigenvalues of \(A\). We derive the Arnoldi relation, show that each
Ritz pair's residual comes for free from the last row of \(H\), and turn that residual into an
error bound. The test matrix is the random walk on a 10-nearest-neighbour graph of the 1,797
images in `dm::datasets::digits()`: sparse, not symmetric, with eigenvalues crowded just
below 1. After 120 matrix-vector products, the twelve largest Ritz values agree with a dense
eigensolver to six decimals or better, and each residual read off \(H\) matches
\(\lVert Ax-\theta x\rVert\) computed directly. The eigenvectors near 1 are the walk's
slow modes: the second isolates the zeros, the third the sixes and the fourth the fours. Building
this example uncovered and fixed a bug in the library's LAPACK bindings.
)

\toc

> Krylov subspaces and the Arnoldi relation

Take an \(n\times n\) matrix \(A\) that we can only multiply by, and a starting vector \(b\).
The *Krylov subspace* of dimension \(m\) is

$$
\mathcal K_m(A,b)=\operatorname{span}\{b,\;Ab,\;A^2b,\;\dots,\;A^{m-1}b\}
=\{\,p(A)\,b : p \text{ a polynomial of degree} < m\,\}.
$$
\alttext(K m of A and b is the span of b, A b, A squared b, up to A to the m minus 1 times b, which is the set of p of A times b for polynomials p of degree less than m.)

It is the largest space we can explore with \(m-1\) products. The power method keeps only
the last vector \(A^{m-1}b\) and so finds only the dominant eigenvector. Krylov methods keep
the whole space. The natural basis \(b, Ab, A^2b,\dots\) is useless in floating point, since
every vector turns toward the dominant eigenvector and the basis becomes nearly dependent.
Arnoldi orthonormalizes as it goes. Starting from \(v_1=b/\lVert b\rVert\), step \(j\)
multiplies the newest basis vector by \(A\), removes its components along \(v_1,\dots,v_j\)
(Gram-Schmidt) and normalizes the remainder:

$$
w=Av_j-\sum_{i=1}^{j}h_{ij}v_i,\qquad h_{ij}=v_i^\top Av_j,\qquad
h_{j+1,j}=\lVert w\rVert,\qquad v_{j+1}=\frac{w}{h_{j+1,j}} .
$$
\alttext(w equals A v j minus the sum over i from 1 to j of h i j times v i, where h i j equals v i transpose A v j. h j plus 1 comma j equals the norm of w, and v j plus 1 equals w over h j plus 1 comma j.)

Each \(v_{j+1}\) is a combination of \(Av_j\) and earlier \(v\)'s, so by induction
\(v_1,\dots,v_m\) span \(\mathcal K_m\). Rearranged, the update says
\(Av_j=\sum_{i=1}^{j+1}h_{ij}v_i\): \(A\) maps the \(j\)-th basis vector into the span of
the first \(j+1\). Collecting the columns \(V_m=[v_1\,\cdots\,v_m]\) and the coefficients into
the \(m\times m\) matrix \(H_m=(h_{ij})\), which is zero below its first subdiagonal (upper
*Hessenberg*), gives the *Arnoldi relation*

$$
AV_m=V_mH_m+h_{m+1,m}\,v_{m+1}e_m^\top,
\qquad\text{so}\qquad H_m=V_m^\top AV_m .
$$
\alttext(A times V m equals V m times H m plus h m plus 1 comma m times v m plus 1 times e m transpose. So H m equals V m transpose A V m.)

The second form follows by multiplying on the left by \(V_m^\top\) and using
\(V_m^\top V_m=I\) and \(V_m^\top v_{m+1}=0\). \(H_m\) is the matrix of \(A\) compressed to
\(\mathcal K_m\): the orthogonal projection of \(A\) onto the subspace, written in the basis
\(V_m\). If some \(h_{j+1,j}\) is zero, \(\mathcal K_j\) is invariant under \(A\) and the
eigenvalues of \(H_j\) are exact eigenvalues of \(A\).

>> Ritz pairs and their free residuals

To approximate eigenpairs, look for \(x=V_my\) in the subspace whose residual is orthogonal
to the subspace (the *Rayleigh-Ritz* or Galerkin condition):

$$
V_m^\top(AV_my-\theta V_my)=0\iff H_my=\theta y .
$$
\alttext(V m transpose times A V m y minus theta V m y equals 0, if and only if H m y equals theta y.)

So the *Ritz values* \(\theta\) are the eigenvalues of the small matrix \(H_m\), and the
*Ritz vectors* are \(x=V_my\). The Arnoldi relation also gives the residual of each pair
without another product with \(A\). Applying the relation to \(y\), with \(\lVert y\rVert=1\):

$$
Ax-\theta x=AV_my-\theta V_my=V_m(H_my-\theta y)+h_{m+1,m}v_{m+1}(e_m^\top y)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \lVert Ax-\theta x\rVert=h_{m+1,m}\,|y_m| .
$$
\alttext(A x minus theta x equals A V m y minus theta V m y, which equals V m times H m y minus theta y, plus h m plus 1 comma m times v m plus 1 times e m transpose y. Therefore the norm of A x minus theta x equals h m plus 1 comma m times the absolute value of y m.)

A Ritz pair has converged when the last component of its eigenvector of \(H_m\) is small.
`dm::algorithms::ritz_pairs()` computes this residual for every pair, and
`arnoldi_iteration()` accepts any matrix-vector product as a function, so \(A\) never
needs to be stored.

> The test matrix: a random walk on the digits

`dm::datasets::digits()` holds 1,797 handwritten digits, each an \(8\times8\) image with
pixel intensities from 0 to 16, so each image is a point in \(\mathbb R^{64}\). We join two
images when either is among the other's 10 nearest neighbours by Euclidean distance. This
gives an undirected graph with adjacency matrix \(W\) and degree matrix \(D\), built by
`dm::stats::KnnGraph`. The *random walk* on the graph steps from an image to a uniformly
chosen neighbour. Its transition matrix is

$$
P=D^{-1}W,\qquad P_{ij}=\frac{W_{ij}}{d_i},\qquad P\mathbf 1=\mathbf 1 .
$$
\alttext(P equals D inverse W, with entries P i j equal to W i j over d i, and P times the all-ones vector equals the all-ones vector.)

\(P\) is sparse and not symmetric. It is, however, similar to a symmetric matrix:
\(P=D^{-1/2}SD^{1/2}\) with \(S=D^{-1/2}WD^{-1/2}\). So its eigenvalues are real, and since
the rows of \(P\) are probability distributions they lie in \([-1,1]\). The eigenvalue 1
appears once for each connected component. Eigenvalues just below 1 belong to *slow modes*:
functions on the images that the walk averages away only slowly. These are close to
constant on groups of images that the walk rarely leaves, so they reveal clusters, which is
the idea behind spectral clustering and diffusion maps. The 10 digit classes should show up
as a crowd of eigenvalues near 1. That crowd is a hard case for Arnoldi, which separates
eigenvalues by their gaps.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> images;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) x.push_back(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r));
    images.push_back(x);
  }
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(images, 10);
  const auto& d = graph.degrees();
  const auto [dmin, dmax] = std::ranges::minmax(d);
  double dmean = 0.0;
  for (double v : d) dmean += v / d.size();

  std::print("| vertices | edges | components | min degree | mean degree | max degree | non-zeros of \\(P\\) | "
             "fill |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.2f} | {:.0f} | {} | {:.2f}% |\n", graph.size(), graph.edges(),
             graph.components(), dmin, dmean, dmax, 2 * graph.edges(),
             100.0 * 2 * graph.edges() / (static_cast<double>(graph.size()) * graph.size()));

  // Sanity checks of the random walk operator: P 1 = 1 and D P is symmetric.
  std::vector<double> ones(graph.size(), 1.0);
  double row_sum_error = 0.0;
  for (double v : graph.random_walk_apply(ones)) row_sum_error = std::max(row_sum_error, std::abs(v - 1.0));
  std::print("\nmax |P 1 - 1| = {:.1e}\n", row_sum_error);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| vertices | edges | components | min degree | mean degree | max degree | non-zeros of \(P\) | fill |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1797 | 12339 | 1 | 10 | 13.73 | 35 | 24678 | 0.76% |

max |P 1 - 1| = 0.0e+00
// result_end

The graph has 12,339 edges, so each image has 13.7 neighbours on average. Every image has
at least its own 10, and a few popular images are chosen by many others, up to 35. The
graph is connected, so the eigenvalue 1 is simple. \(P\) has 24,678 non-zero entries, 0.76%
of a dense \(1797\times1797\) matrix, and its rows sum to one exactly.

> Running Arnoldi

We start from a random vector (not \(\mathbf 1\), which is already an eigenvector), run
\(m=120\) steps of `arnoldi_iteration()` with the walk as a matrix-free product, and check
the two promises of the construction. The basis should be orthonormal and the Arnoldi
relation should hold, column by column, up to rounding. The table shows the top-left corner
of \(H_{120}\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> images;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) x.push_back(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r));
    images.push_back(x);
  }
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(images, 10);
  const alg::MatVec walk = [&](const std::vector<double>& x) { return graph.random_walk_apply(x); };

  std::mt19937_64 rng(7);
  std::normal_distribution<double> z;
  std::vector<double> b(graph.size());
  for (double& v : b) v = z(rng);

  const auto arnoldi = alg::arnoldi_iteration(walk, b, 120);
  std::print("basis vectors: {}, H: {} x {}\n\n", arnoldi.V.size(), arnoldi.H.size(), arnoldi.H[0].size());
  std::print("| check | value |\n| --- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| \\(\\max|V^\\top V-I|\\) | {:.1e} |\n", alg::arnoldi_orthogonality_loss(arnoldi));
  std::print("| \\(\\max_j\\lVert Pv_j-\\sum_i h_{{ij}}v_i\\rVert\\) | {:.1e} |\n",
             alg::arnoldi_relation_residual(walk, arnoldi));
  double below = 0.0, asym = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 120; ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 120; ++j) {
      if (i > j + 1) below = std::max(below, std::abs(arnoldi.H[i][j]));
      if (i <= j + 1) asym = std::max(asym, std::abs(arnoldi.H[i][j] - arnoldi.H[j][i]));
    }
  std::print("| largest entry below the subdiagonal | {:.1e} |\n", below);
  std::print("| largest \\(|h_{{ij}}-h_{{ji}}|\\) | {:.3f} |\n\n", asym);

  std::print("| \\(H\\) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
    std::print("| {} |", i + 1);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) std::print(" {:.4f} |", arnoldi.H[i][j]);
    std::print("\n");
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
basis vectors: 121, H: 121 x 120

| check | value |
| --- | ---: |
| \(\max|V^\top V-I|\) | 5.6e-15 |
| \(\max_j\lVert Pv_j-\sum_i h_{ij}v_i\rVert\) | 2.0e-17 |
| largest entry below the subdiagonal | 0.0e+00 |
| largest \(|h_{ij}-h_{ji}|\) | 0.014 |

| \(H\) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | -0.0156 | 0.2480 | -0.0030 | -0.0053 | -0.0016 | 0.0004 |
| 2 | 0.2613 | 0.3253 | 0.3762 | 0.0067 | 0.0028 | 0.0005 |
| 3 | 0.0000 | 0.3701 | 0.3235 | 0.3602 | -0.0016 | 0.0032 |
| 4 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.3629 | 0.3263 | 0.3454 | 0.0010 |
| 5 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.3452 | 0.2955 | 0.3643 |
| 6 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.3658 | 0.3314 |
// result_end

Both promises hold to rounding error: the 121 basis vectors are orthonormal to
\(6\times10^{-15}\), and each column of the Arnoldi relation holds to \(2\times10^{-17}\).
\(H\) is upper Hessenberg by construction. It is *not* symmetric, with \(h_{ij}\) and
\(h_{ji}\) differing by up to 0.014, because \(P\) is not symmetric in the ordinary inner
product that Arnoldi uses. It is nearly tridiagonal, though, with small entries above the
superdiagonal. That is the trace of \(P\)'s hidden symmetry: for a symmetric matrix
\(H_m=V_m^\top AV_m\) is symmetric and Hessenberg, hence tridiagonal, and Arnoldi becomes
the Lanczos iteration. The first diagonal entry \(h_{11}=v_1^\top Pv_1=-0.016\) is the
Rayleigh quotient of the random start, which is dominated by fast, oscillating modes.

> Watching the Ritz values converge

For a reference we compute every eigenvalue of \(P\) with a dense symmetric solver applied
to \(S\) (`KnnGraph::random_walk_spectrum()`). This costs \(O(n^3)\) operations and
\(n^2\) memory, which an iterative method is meant to avoid. The figure plots, for each
\(m\) from 1 to 120, the twelve largest Ritz values against the twelve largest eigenvalues.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arnoldi_iteration_convergence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> images;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) x.push_back(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r));
    images.push_back(x);
  }
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(images, 10);
  const alg::MatVec walk = [&](const std::vector<double>& x) { return graph.random_walk_apply(x); };
  std::mt19937_64 rng(7);
  std::normal_distribution<double> z;
  std::vector<double> b(graph.size());
  for (double& v : b) v = z(rng);

  const auto exact = graph.random_walk_spectrum();
  const auto full = alg::arnoldi_iteration(walk, b, 120);

  std::vector<double> m_axis, theta;
  double max_imag = 0.0;
  for (int m = 1; m <= 120; ++m) {
    alg::ArnoldiResult r{std::vector(full.V.begin(), full.V.begin() + m + 1), {}};
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; ++i) r.H.emplace_back(full.H[i].begin(), full.H[i].begin() + m);
    const auto pairs = alg::ritz_pairs(r, false);
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < pairs.size() && k < 12; ++k) {
      if (pairs[k].value.real() < 0.85) continue;
      m_axis.push_back(m);
      theta.push_back(pairs[k].value.real());
    }
    for (const auto& p : pairs) max_imag = std::max(max_imag, std::abs(p.value.imag()));
  }

  const auto pairs = alg::ritz_pairs(full);
  std::print("| \\(k\\) | eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_k\\) | Ritz value \\(\\theta_k\\) (\\(m=120\\)) | "
             "\\(|\\theta_k-\\lambda_k|\\) | residual \\(h_{{m+1,m}}|y_m|\\) | direct \\(\\lVert Px-\\theta x\\rVert\\) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 14; ++k) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (const auto& c : pairs[k].vector) x.push_back(c.real());
    const auto px = walk(x);
    double direct = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) direct += std::pow(px[i] - pairs[k].value.real() * x[i], 2);
    std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.1e} | {:.2e} | {:.2e} |\n", k + 1, exact[k], pairs[k].value.real(),
               std::abs(pairs[k].value.real() - exact[k]), pairs[k].residual, std::sqrt(direct));
  }
  std::print("\nLargest |imaginary part| of any Ritz value for m = 1..120: {:.1e}\n", max_imag);
  std::print("Smallest eigenvalue: {:.4f}; most negative Ritz value at m = 120: ", exact.back());
  double low = 0.0;
  for (const auto& p : pairs) low = std::min(low, p.value.real());
  std::print("{:.4f}\n", low);

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 12; ++k) plot.abline_h(exact[k], dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 0.8);
  plot.points(m_axis, theta, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
      .y_limits(0.85, 1.005)
      .title("Twelve largest Ritz values of the digits random walk")
      .x_label("Arnoldi steps m")
      .y_label("Ritz value")
      .size(900, 520)
      .save_svg("build/arnoldi_iteration_convergence.svg");
}
```
