Smallest eigenvalue: -0.3931; most negative Ritz value at m = 120: -0.3931
\image(figures/arnoldi_iteration_convergence.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Blue points: the twelve largest Ritz values after \(m\) Arnoldi steps (values below 0.85 are off the scale). Red lines: the twelve largest eigenvalues of the random walk, from a dense eigensolver.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of Ritz values between 0.85 and 1 against the number of Arnoldi steps from 1 to 120, with twelve red horizontal lines at the eigenvalues between 0.945 and 1. Rows of blue points climb from below as m grows and level off on the red lines one after another. The line at 0.945 is reached by about m equals 45, the highest lines between about m equals 35 and 80, and the crowded lines between 0.96 and 0.99 by m equals 80 to 100. By m equals 120 there is a level row of points on every red line, and further rows are still climbing toward 0.93.)

Each Ritz value climbs toward the spectrum from below and then locks onto an eigenvalue. The
order in which they converge depends on separation more than on size. The eigenvalue 0.945
is relatively isolated, with gaps of 0.010 and 0.017 to its neighbours, and is matched by
about \(m=45\). The crowded eigenvalues between 0.96 and 0.995 need 80 to 100 steps. Even
the top eigenvalue 1 has a gap of only 0.003 to the next. It is matched to four decimals
around \(m=60\) and to five by \(m=80\). At \(m=120\) the twelve largest Ritz values agree with the dense
eigenvalues to \(3\times10^{-7}\) or better, the 13th is still off by
\(3\times10^{-6}\), and the bottom of the spectrum is found too: the most negative Ritz
value equals the smallest eigenvalue, \(-0.3931\). Arnoldi converges to both ends at once.

The two residual columns agree to every printed digit, as the derivation promised: the
residual from the last row of \(H\) costs nothing, while the direct one needs another
product with \(P\). Although \(H\) is not symmetric, no Ritz value had an imaginary part
in any of the 120 runs, consistent with the real spectrum of \(P\).

>> From residual to error

The residual is computable, but the error \(|\theta-\lambda|\) is what we want. For a
diagonalizable \(A=X\Lambda X^{-1}\), the *Bauer-Fike* argument bounds one by the other. If
\(\theta\) is not an eigenvalue, write \(r=Ax-\theta x\), so
\(x=X(\Lambda-\theta I)^{-1}X^{-1}r\). Taking norms, with \(\lVert x\rVert=1\):

$$
1\le\lVert X\rVert\,\lVert X^{-1}\rVert\,\max_k\frac{1}{|\lambda_k-\theta|}\,\lVert r\rVert
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\min_k|\lambda_k-\theta|\le\kappa(X)\,\lVert r\rVert .
$$
\alttext(1 is at most the norm of X times the norm of X inverse times the maximum over k of 1 over the absolute value of lambda k minus theta, times the norm of r. Therefore the minimum over k of the absolute value of lambda k minus theta is at most kappa of X times the norm of r.)

For the random walk, \(P=D^{-1/2}SD^{1/2}\) and \(S\) has orthonormal eigenvectors \(Q\), so
\(X=D^{-1/2}Q\) and \(\kappa(X)\le\sqrt{d_{\max}/d_{\min}}\). With degrees between 10
and 35 this is \(\sqrt{3.5}=1.87\). Every Ritz value is therefore within \(\sqrt{3.5}=1.87\) times its residual of
some eigenvalue of \(P\), a guarantee that needs no dense solve. For a symmetric matrix
\(\kappa=1\), and a sharper argument gives a bound in terms of the squared residual. The
table above shows how conservative the linear bound is.

In the table the actual errors are far below this bound, by factors of 80 to 3,600. For
\(\theta_1\) the residual is \(2.7\times10^{-10}\) but the error is only
\(1.3\times10^{-12}\). Errors that small are what the symmetric theory predicts: for a
self-adjoint matrix the error in an isolated eigenvalue is of order
\(\lVert r\rVert^2/\text{gap}\), and \(P\) is self-adjoint in the inner product weighted
by \(D\). So the linear bound is a safe stopping rule, and the actual accuracy is usually
much better.

> The slow modes of the walk

The Ritz vectors for the eigenvalues just below 1 are the walk's slow modes. Up to the
\(D^{1/2}\) scaling they are the coordinates used by spectral clustering and diffusion maps.
We plot every image at its values in the second and third Ritz vectors, coloured by the
digit it shows, and summarize each digit's position with the `dm` pipe verbs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arnoldi_iteration_modes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;
namespace agg = dm::agg;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> images;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) x.push_back(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r));
    images.push_back(x);
  }
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(images, 10);
  const alg::MatVec walk = [&](const std::vector<double>& x) { return graph.random_walk_apply(x); };
  std::mt19937_64 rng(7);
  std::normal_distribution<double> z;
  std::vector<double> b(graph.size());
  for (double& v : b) v = z(rng);
  const auto pairs = alg::ritz_pairs(alg::arnoldi_iteration(walk, b, 120));

  // Scale each slow mode to unit root-mean-square, sign chosen so the zeros sit at positive values.
  auto mode = [&](std::size_t k) {
    std::vector<double> v;
    for (const auto& c : pairs[k].vector) v.push_back(c.real() * std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(graph.size())));
    double zeros = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)
      if (digits.double_at("digit", i) == 0) zeros += v[i];
    if (zeros < 0)
      for (double& x : v) x = -x;
    return v;
  };
  const auto u2 = mode(1), u3 = mode(2), u4 = mode(3);
  auto frame = digits;
  frame.add_column("mode_2", u2);
  frame.add_column("mode_3", u3);
  frame.add_column("mode_4", u4);
  std::print("{}\n", frame | dm::group_by("digit")
                           | dm::summarise(agg::n("images"), agg::mean("mode_2", "mean mode 2"),
                                           agg::mean("mode_3", "mean mode 3"), agg::mean("mode_4", "mean mode 4"),
                                           agg::sd("mode_2", "sd mode 2"))
                           | dm::to_md(2));

  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours = {{31, 119, 180}, {255, 127, 14}, {44, 160, 44},  {214, 39, 40},
                                        {148, 103, 189}, {140, 86, 75},  {227, 119, 194}, {127, 127, 127},
                                        {188, 189, 34},  {23, 190, 207}};
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<std::string> names;
  for (int d = 0; d < 10; ++d) {
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < u2.size(); ++i)
      if (digits.double_at("digit", i) == d) {
        x.push_back(u2[i]);
        y.push_back(u3[i]);
      }
    plot.points(x, y, std::to_string(d), colours[d], 3.5);
    names.push_back(std::to_string(d));
  }
  plot.legend(names, colours)
      .x_limits(-1, 4.6)
      .y_limits(-3.2, 2.6)
      .title("Images at their second and third slow modes")
      .x_label("Ritz vector 2 (scaled)")
      .y_label("Ritz vector 3 (scaled)")
      .size(900, 640)
      .save_svg("build/arnoldi_iteration_modes.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| digit | images | mean mode 2 | mean mode 3 | mean mode 4 | sd mode 2 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 178 | 2.94 | 0.39 | 0.17 | 0.13 |
| 1 | 182 | -0.39 | -0.18 | 0.39 | 0.11 |
| 2 | 177 | -0.27 | 0.28 | 1.59 | 0.12 |
| 3 | 183 | -0.46 | 0.35 | 0.47 | 0.03 |
| 4 | 181 | -0.12 | 0.46 | -2.10 | 0.07 |
| 5 | 182 | -0.42 | -0.17 | -0.16 | 0.04 |
| 6 | 181 | 0.06 | -2.65 | -0.63 | 0.07 |
| 7 | 179 | -0.54 | 0.77 | -0.23 | 0.01 |
| 8 | 174 | -0.47 | 0.33 | 0.29 | 0.02 |
| 9 | 180 | -0.49 | 0.35 | 0.26 | 0.02 |

\image(figures/arnoldi_iteration_modes.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Each point is one of the 1,797 images, placed by its entries in the second and third Ritz vectors (scaled to unit root-mean-square) and coloured by its digit.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of 1,797 points coloured by digit 0 to 9, with Ritz vector 2 on the horizontal axis from minus 1 to 4.6 and Ritz vector 3 on the vertical axis from minus 3.2 to 2.6. The zeros form a separate cluster near x equals 3, y equals 0.4. The sixes form a separate cluster near x equals 0, y equals minus 2.7, with a few ones next to them. All other digits are packed into a band near x equals minus 0.5 and y between minus 0.5 and 0.8, where the fours and twos extend slightly to the right.)

The second slow mode is a zero detector. Images of 0 average 2.94 on it, with a standard
deviation of 0.13, while every other digit averages between \(-0.54\) and 0.06. The third
mode does the same for the sixes, at \(-2.65\), along with a few ones that sit next to
them. The fourth mode, not plotted, picks out the fours (\(-2.10\)) and, at its other end,
the twos (1.59). Each slow mode marks a group of images with few edges to the rest of the
graph, where a random walker stays for a long time. Zeros come first because a round,
closed stroke resembles no other digit. The remaining digits overlap in these coordinates
and are separated only by further modes. This is why spectral clustering uses about as
many eigenvectors as there are clusters, here the ten or so eigenvalues above 0.96.

> Cost

The point of a Krylov method is that it touches \(P\) only through products. Each product
costs one pass over the 24,678 non-zeros, and the Arnoldi loop adds the Gram-Schmidt work,
\(O(nm)\) per step and \(O(nm^2)\) in total. A dense eigensolver needs \(O(n^3)\)
operations and the whole \(n\times n\) matrix in memory.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> images;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r) {
    std::vector<double> x;
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) x.push_back(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r));
    images.push_back(x);
  }
  using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
  auto ms = [](clock::time_point a, clock::time_point b) { return std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(b - a).count(); };

  const auto t0 = clock::now();
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(images, 10);
  const auto t1 = clock::now();
  std::size_t products = 0;
  const alg::MatVec walk = [&](const std::vector<double>& x) { ++products; return graph.random_walk_apply(x); };
  std::mt19937_64 rng(7);
  std::normal_distribution<double> z;
  std::vector<double> b(graph.size());
  for (double& v : b) v = z(rng);
  const auto t2 = clock::now();
  const auto arnoldi = alg::arnoldi_iteration(walk, b, 120);
  const auto t3 = clock::now();
  const auto pairs = alg::ritz_pairs(arnoldi);
  const auto t4 = clock::now();
  const auto exact = graph.random_walk_spectrum();
  const auto t5 = clock::now();

  const double n = static_cast<double>(graph.size());
  std::print("| step | time (ms) | memory for the matrix or basis |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| build the 10-NN graph (brute force) | {:.0f} | {:.0f} kB (sparse \\(P\\)) |\n", ms(t0, t1),
             (2.0 * graph.edges() * 8 + (n + 1) * 8 + n * 8) / 1024);
  std::print("| Arnoldi, \\(m=120\\) ({} products with \\(P\\)) | {:.0f} | {:.0f} kB (basis \\(V\\)) |\n", products,
             ms(t2, t3), 121 * n * 8 / 1024);
  std::print("| Ritz pairs from \\(H_{{120}}\\) | {:.0f} | {:.0f} kB |\n", ms(t3, t4), 120.0 * 120 * 8 / 1024);
  std::print("| dense symmetric eigensolver on \\(S\\) | {:.0f} | {:.0f} kB (dense \\(S\\)) |\n", ms(t4, t5),
             n * n * 8 / 1024);
  std::print("\n{} eigenvalues from the dense solver, {} Ritz values.\n", exact.size(), pairs.size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| step | time (ms) | memory for the matrix or basis |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| build the 10-NN graph (brute force) | 60 | 221 kB (sparse \(P\)) |
| Arnoldi, \(m=120\) (120 products with \(P\)) | 9 | 1699 kB (basis \(V\)) |
| Ritz pairs from \(H_{120}\) | 24 | 112 kB |
| dense symmetric eigensolver on \(S\) | 264 | 25228 kB (dense \(S\)) |

1797 eigenvalues from the dense solver, 120 Ritz values.
// result_end

On this machine 120 Arnoldi steps take about 10 milliseconds, and the Ritz pairs, including
the 120 Ritz vectors \(V_my\), about as long again. The dense solver, using LAPACK,
takes about 200 milliseconds and 25 MB. The gap grows quickly with \(n\). For a graph of a
million images the dense matrix would need 8 terabytes, while 120 Arnoldi vectors would
need about 1 GB, and the products would still cost time proportional to the number of
edges. Arnoldi does not give the whole spectrum, only the extreme eigenvalues, but those
are usually the ones wanted.

> Summary

- Arnoldi builds an orthonormal basis \(V_m\) of the Krylov subspace and the Hessenberg
  matrix \(H_m=V_m^\top AV_m\) from \(m\) products with \(A\). On the 1,797-image random
  walk the basis stayed orthonormal to \(6\times10^{-15}\).
- The eigenvalues of \(H_m\) approximate the extreme eigenvalues of \(A\), and the residual
  of each pair, \(h_{m+1,m}|y_m|\), comes free from the last row of \(H\). It matched the
  directly computed residual to every printed digit.
- Bauer-Fike turns the residual into an error bound, \(\kappa(X)\lVert r\rVert\) with
  \(\kappa\le1.87\) here. The actual errors were 80 to 3,600 times smaller.
- Isolated eigenvalues converge first. After 120 steps, the twelve largest eigenvalues of
  the walk, all crowded into \([0.945,1]\), were correct to \(3\times10^{-7}\).
- The slow modes of the walk separate the zeros, sixes and fours from the other digits.
  That is the basis of spectral clustering.

>> A bug found along the way

The Ritz values come from `nn::eig`, which calls LAPACK's `dgeev` when the library is built
with OpenBLAS. The first run of this report printed
`On entry to DGEEV parameter number 11 had an illegal value`, and the eigenvalues-only call
crashed. The OpenBLAS in this build environment uses 64-bit integers in its interface, but
`datamunge` declared every LAPACK integer argument as a 32-bit `int`. LAPACK therefore read
each size and leading dimension as 8 bytes, of which 4 were whatever happened to sit next to
it in memory. The other LAPACK paths (SVD, symmetric eigensolver, QR, Cholesky, LU) passed
their integers the same way and could read garbage just as easily. The library now uses 64-bit LAPACK integers whenever OpenBLAS reports
`OPENBLAS_USE64BITINT`. The tests that compare the LAPACK results with the built-in kernels
pass, and a test of complex Ritz values was added.
